.PP
Send sig to p (to its whole process group if it was started with newGroup or joined one with pgid). false if p has already been reaped or kill fails.
.SS communicate
.EX
i64 communicate(Proc *p, u8[] input, String *out=null, String *err=null);
.EE
.PP
Write input (nothing if it is empty) to p's stdin and close it, read p's stdout and stderr until both reach EOF, then wait for p. *out / *err get what was read as a new String (yours; what was in them is replaced, not freed), empty with a null ptr for a stream that isn't a PIPE; pass null to read and drop a stream. A child that exits without reading all of its input is fine: the rest is dropped. Returns what wait returns.
.SS release
.EX
void release(Proc *p);
.EE
.PP
Close our pipe ends and free p (null is ignored). A child that is still running is waited for: proc.kill it first to stop it.
.SS disown
.EX
void disown(Proc *p);
.EE
.PP
Close our pipe ends and free p without waiting for it: for a program that reaps its children itself (a shell's waitpid(-1), coro.waitChild on p.pid). Keep p.pid first if you need it.
.SS run
.EX
String run(u8 **argv, i64 *code=null);
.EE
.PP
Run argv (no shell; stdin /dev/null, stderr inherited) and return what it printed on stdout as a new String (yours), with one trailing newline removed; its exit code (as wait) in *code. Empty with a null ptr if it couldn't be started (os.errno() says why).
.SS shell
.EX
i64 shell(u8[] command);
.EE
.PP
Run \fBcommand\fR with the shell (\fBsh \-c\fR). Returns the command's exit code (0-255), or -1 if it was killed by a signal or the shell could not be run. The shell itself gives 127 for a command it can't find.
.SS shellf
.EX
i32 shellf(u8 *fmt, ...args);
.EE
.PP
Call the shell with \fBprintf\fR-like formatting. Returns what System returns: the exit code (0-255), or -1 if the command was killed by a signal, could not be run, or fmt is null.
.SS shellQuote
.EX
String shellQuote(u8[] s);
.EE
.PP
s in single quotes for sh, as a new String (yours): each ' becomes '\e'' and an empty s gives ''. Nothing in it is expanded by the shell, so proc.shellf("ls %s", q) passes s as one word.
.SS shellOutput
.EX
String shellOutput(u8 *fmt, ...args);
.EE
.PP
Run a shell command, built with \fBprintf\fR-like formatting, and return what it printed to stdout as a new String (yours). One trailing newline is removed. The exit status is not returned: a failing command gives whatever it printed, often "". stderr is not captured. Returns when the shell exits, not waiting for background jobs it started. An empty String with a null ptr if fmt is null or the shell can't be started.
